"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,175\nThe Learning Brain. Series 7: Digital Learning\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,416\nEpisode 11\n\n3\n00:00:07,496 --> 00:00:09,541\nLecture recordings\n\n4\n00:00:12,341 --> 00:00:16,039\nLecture recordings are now standard at many universities.\n\n5\n00:00:16,350 --> 00:00:17,618\nStudents love them.\n\n6\n00:00:17,999 --> 00:00:19,713\nBut do they change who shows up,\n\n7\n00:00:19,752 --> 00:00:21,165\nand what students learn?\n\n8\n00:00:21,335 --> 00:00:23,350\nIn a 2019 study,\n\n9\n00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:32,913\nMartin Edwards and Michael Clinton compared two matched groups in the same course: one hundred sixty one students before recordings were introduced,\n\n10\n00:00:33,152 --> 00:00:35,017\nand one hundred sixty after.\n\n11\n00:00:35,187 --> 00:00:39,103\nAfter recordings arrived, attendance dropped substantially.\n\n12\n00:00:39,603 --> 00:00:41,537\nAttendance predicted grades.\n\n13\n00:00:41,804 --> 00:00:45,754\nAnd watching recordings did not make up for the lectures students missed.\n\n14\n00:00:46,196 --> 00:00:47,822\nThe authors concluded that,\n\n15\n00:00:47,904 --> 00:00:51,148\nfor this group, the net effect was generally negative.\n\n16\n00:00:51,318 --> 00:00:57,165\nA 2019 study by Emily Nordmann and colleagues found a more nuanced picture.\n\n17\n00:00:57,748 --> 00:01:01,974\nHigh achieving students could use recordings to make up for missed lectures.\n\n18\n00:01:02,373 --> 00:01:08,446\nLower achieving students benefited only when they used recordings on top of the lectures they attended.\n\n19\n00:01:08,979 --> 00:01:10,003\nAnd in that study,\n\n20\n00:01:10,249 --> 00:01:12,865\nrecordings did not reduce attendance.\n\n21\n00:01:13,035 --> 00:01:14,534\nRecordings still matter.\n\n22\n00:01:14,956 --> 00:01:17,160\nThey support students with disabilities,\n\n23\n00:01:17,265 --> 00:01:19,460\nstudents who are ill or working,\n\n24\n00:01:19,623 --> 00:01:21,913\nand anyone reviewing a hard section.\n\n25\n00:01:22,410 --> 00:01:24,365\nThe problem is not the recording.\n\n26\n00:01:24,745 --> 00:01:27,441\nIt is using it as a replacement for class,\n\n27\n00:01:27,651 --> 00:01:30,270\noften in a late binge before an exam.\n\n28\n00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:32,841\nBinge watching is massed practice,\n\n29\n00:01:33,066 --> 00:01:34,880\nand as we saw in Series one,\n\n30\n00:01:34,987 --> 00:01:36,261\nspacing works better.\n\n31\n00:01:36,779 --> 00:01:40,542\nOne long cram is the least effective way to use a recording.\n\n32\n00:01:40,712 --> 00:01:41,471\nFor teaching.\n\n33\n00:01:41,842 --> 00:01:48,070\nKeep recordings, and say plainly how to use them: to review and fill gaps,\n\n34\n00:01:48,449 --> 00:01:50,424\nnot to replace attending.\n\n35\n00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:53,309\nMake class time worth attending,\n\n36\n00:01:53,715 --> 00:01:56,562\nwith activities a recording cannot capture.\n\n37\n00:01:56,920 --> 00:01:58,820\nSuggest a weekly review rather than a binge.\n\n38\n00:01:59,057 --> 00:02:01,410\nOne tip to share with students: use recordings to review,\n\n39\n00:02:01,430 --> 00:02:02,475\nnot to replace class.\n\n40\n00:02:02,645 --> 00:02:04,305\nA recording keeps the lecture.\n\n41\n00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:06,587\nOnly attending keeps the class.\n\n"